  "/faq/screeding-calculator": [
    {
      "q": "How much sand and cement do I need for a floor screed?",
      "a": "Multiply the floor area by the thickness to get a volume, then split that volume by the mix ratio into sand and cement. The calculator does both steps and expresses cement in the bag size sold in your market, with sand by volume or by tonne. Two things move the answer more than the area does: the thickness, which multiplies everything, and the mix, because a one to three screed uses noticeably more cement than a one to five for the same volume."
    },
    {
      "q": "How thick should a floor screed be?",
      "a": "Between forty and seventy five millimetres is common for a levelling screed over a prepared base, with thinner sections used for a wearing surface over a sound floor. Below about forty millimetres the risk of curling and debonding rises, and above seventy five the material cost climbs quickly. Those are specification judgements, so the calculator asks for your thickness and shows what it consumes rather than choosing one for you."
    },
    {
      "q": "Does a screeding estimate include the floor finish?",
      "a": "No. Tile, stone, screed paint and any levelling compound above the screed are separate products with their own quantities and their own trades. The estimate covers the screed itself, which is what you order before the floor finish is even selected. Keeping them apart matters on a quotation, because a client changing the tile should not change the screed quantity, and mixing them makes that comparison impossible."
    },
    {
      "q": "What thickness should I enter for screeding?",
      "a": "Enter the depth the specification calls for, in millimetres. If the base is uneven, the average depth is what governs the order, so measure the high and low points and use a figure that reflects the average rather than the minimum. The calculator shows the volume implied, which is the quickest way to sanity check whether the depth on a drawing meets the floor level you are trying to achieve."
    },
    {
      "q": "Does screeding cover a floor or a wall?",
      "a": "Both, as separate surfaces, because they are measured and bought differently. A floor screed is a volume of sand and cement placed to a depth; a wall render is an area at a stated thickness, often with a different mix and a different finish. The calculator keeps the two apart so a wall is not ordered by the cubic metre and a floor is not priced from a render rate per square metre."
    }
  ],
