  "/faq/foundation-calculator": [
    {
      "q": "How much concrete do I need for a foundation?",
      "a": "Multiply each footing run's length, width and depth, add the runs, then add the waste allowance you work to. The calculator does that from the dimensions you enter and shows the total before waste as well as after, so a checking engineer can see both. Cement, sand and coarse aggregate then follow from the mix you specify, expressed in the bag size and the units your market sells, so the order is something a supplier can price."
    },
    {
      "q": "How many blocks do I need for the foundation?",
      "a": "Take the wall length, multiply by the height, divide by the face area of the block you are using, then deduct the openings and add waste. The calculator works from the block size you specify, which matters because a four hundred and fifty by two hundred and twenty five millimetre block and a four hundred and forty by two hundred and fifteen millimetre block do not cover the same area. Mortar follows the joint thickness and the wall area, so a thinner joint reduces both the mortar and the cement in it."
    },
    {
      "q": "Is this foundation design?",
      "a": "No, and it says so on the page. Footing width, depth and reinforcement are structural design decisions that follow from the load and the ground, and a quantity tool cannot make them. When those dimensions come from your engineer, this tool converts them into a material order. When they do not exist yet, the useful next step is a soil report and a design, not a guessed footing width."
    },
    {
      "q": "Does the foundation estimate include formwork?",
      "a": "No. Formwork, hardcore, blinding, damp proofing and excavation are separate items with their own quantities, and they are usually priced by a different trade. The estimate covers the concrete and the blockwork, which are the two materials you need to order before the first pour. Adding formwork to a concrete quantity produces a figure that reads as complete while leaving several real costs out of it."
    },
    {
      "q": "Does the foundation cost include tax?",
      "a": "No. Tax is not determined in this estimate. Where a configured tax rule applies to your market, it appears as its own line with the basis stated; where no rule applies, the total is shown without tax rather than with an assumed percentage. That matters when you are comparing quotes from different regions, because a figure with an invented tax rate cannot be compared with one without it."
    }
  ],
