  "/faq/build-to-roof-estimator": [
    {
      "q": "What is a build-to-roof estimator?",
      "a": "A build to roof estimator prices the stages from the footings up to the roof covering: footing concrete, blockwork and mortar, roof members, sheeting and the eaves trim. It is a stage estimate, not a whole house estimate, and that boundary is the point. Builders use it to answer the question that comes before every other one, which is whether there is enough money to get the building closed and dry."
    },
    {
      "q": "How are roofing sheets calculated from a roof area?",
      "a": "From the roof area, adjusted for pitch, then divided by the cover width of the sheet you are using and rounded up for the length of each run. Laps are part of the calculation, because a sheet that overhangs the eaves or laps the next one covers less than its nominal width. Enter the cover width the manufacturer states and the sheet length you will order, and the count comes back in whole sheets rather than square metres."
    },
    {
      "q": "Where does the eaves perimeter come from?",
      "a": "From the plan dimensions of the building, since the eaves perimeter follows the wall line plus whatever overhang you set. It is not the same as the roof perimeter at the pitch, and it is not the wall perimeter at floor level, because both differ once there is an overhang. Fascia, gutter and soffit are all ordered from this figure, which is why a roof area alone cannot produce them."
    },
    {
      "q": "Where does the build to roof estimate stop?",
      "a": "It stops at the roof covering. Windows, doors, finishes, plumbing, electrical, ceilings and external works are outside it, as are scaffolding, plant and labour. The stage boundary is deliberate: a number that runs into finishes has to make assumptions about specification, and those assumptions are where an estimate stops being checkable. The next stage is priced with its own tools on the same building dimensions."
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does the cement bag count change when I change the mix ratio?",
      "a": "Because cement content is a property of the mix, not of the concrete volume. A one to two to four mix and a one to one and a half to three mix produce visibly different cement quantities from the same cubic metres, while sand and aggregate move in the other direction. Change the mix on the page and the bag count moves with it, which is what lets you compare a specification change against its material cost before you commit to it."
    }
  ],
