  "/faq/soffit-gutter-cost-estimator": [
    {
      "q": "How much gutter and fascia do I need for a roof?",
      "a": "Measure the eaves perimeter, which is the wall line plus the overhang on every side, then add the fittings the run needs: corners, stop ends, outlets and joints. The estimator takes the plan dimensions and the overhang, returns the perimeter, and counts the fittings from your geometry. Gutter is not scaled from roof area, because a shallow roof and a steep roof of the same area have the same gutter and very different areas."
    },
    {
      "q": "How many downpipes does a roof need?",
      "a": "From the roof plan area, the perimeter and the spacing you set. Each downpipe serves a length of gutter, so a long run needs more outlets, and the catchments either side are what the pipe has to carry. The estimator places them around the perimeter at the spacing you choose and counts the pipe, the brackets and the shoe for each. Whether that spacing is adequate is a drainage calculation that depends on the rainfall intensity where you are building."
    },
    {
      "q": "How much soffit area does an overhang create?",
      "a": "The overhang depth multiplied by the eaves perimeter. A six hundred millimetre overhang on a forty two metre perimeter is twenty five square metres of soffit board, and the same roof with a three hundred millimetre overhang is half of that. The area is a flat measure of a sloping surface only where the soffit is horizontal, which most are, and where it is raked the depth you enter is the width of the board rather than a vertical drop."
    },
    {
      "q": "Will the fascia length match my roof estimate?",
      "a": "It should, when both are measured from the same plan. The fascia runs around the same eaves line that the roof edge follows, so the numbers agree once the overhang is included in both. Where they disagree, the usual cause is a roof estimate built from the roof surface at the pitch and a fascia measured from the wall line, which differ by the overhang and by the pitch."
    },
    {
      "q": "How is downpipe spacing decided?",
      "a": "By the length of gutter each downpipe serves. A common guide is one outlet per twelve to fifteen metres of gutter on a simple roof, closer on a valley or where two roofs meet, and always at the low end of a fall. The estimator takes your spacing and counts the pipe, but the adequacy of that spacing depends on the rainfall the roof will actually see, which varies enough by region that it is a design input rather than a default."
    }
  ],
