  "/faq/electrical": [
    {
      "q": "How do I estimate electrical materials for a building?",
      "a": "Count the points and measure the routes. The estimate takes your schedule of sockets, switches, light positions and outlets, multiplies each by the run you measured, and returns cable by size, conduit, boxes, plates and the board. Two inputs drive everything: the number of points, which sets the accessories, and the average or measured route, which sets the cable. A layout that is not yet marked up cannot produce a cable figure, and the tool says so."
    },
    {
      "q": "Can I use the electrical estimate instead of an electrician?",
      "a": "No, and the page says so. Cable size follows current, protection and volt drop; circuits follow the load and the wiring regulations where you are building; testing and certification follow the installation. The estimate measures materials for a design someone else has made. What it gives you is an order you can price before work starts, which is a different document from a certificate."
    },
    {
      "q": "Why is the cable quantity different from what my electrician measured?",
      "a": "Usually because the route differs. An electrician measuring on site follows the actual path through joists, around obstructions and down the wall, and often adds a margin by habit. The estimate uses the length you enter, so if your average run is a straight line between board and point, it will be short. Enter the length you would actually pull, including the drop at each end, and the two numbers converge."
    },
    {
      "q": "How do I count points for a wiring estimate?",
      "a": "Count what you are installing, not what a schedule of rates assumes for a room. Each socket, switch, light position and fixed outlet is a point with its own run, and the count converts into cable, conduit, boxes and plates. Two rooms with the same floor area can differ by ten points, which is the difference the estimate is built to carry. Counting from the drawing also makes the estimate checkable against the second fix."
    },
    {
      "q": "Does the cable length include the drop to each point?",
      "a": "Yes, when you enter a measured route. The distance is from the distribution board to the point along the path the cable takes, which includes the climb from the board and the drop from ceiling level to a socket. A route measured along the wall at socket height understates the cable by the drop, which on a two storey building is a meaningful number of metres across the whole job."
    }
  ],
