  "/faq/conduit-wiring-estimator": [
    {
      "q": "How much conduit do I need for a house?",
      "a": "Measure the runs. Conduit is bought by length, so the only figure that matters is the path each run takes from the board through the building to its last point, plus the drops at each end. An average of nine metres over twenty odd points is a typical flat; a larger house with long corridors and two floors is nearer twelve. The estimator multiplies your measured runs by their count and adds the boxes and couplings the joints need."
    },
    {
      "q": "How do I know which cable size to use?",
      "a": "From the circuit design, not from the estimate. Cable size follows the current the circuit carries, the protective device, the grouping and the volt drop, and every one of those is a design decision made against the wiring regulations where you are building. The estimator asks you what size you are pulling and adds the lengths by size, so the cable order matches the design. Using the tool to choose a size would be using a measuring tape as a rule book."
    },
    {
      "q": "Are junction boxes and switch plates included?",
      "a": "Yes, both are counted. Boxes follow the points, with a box at every accessory and at every joint, and switch plates follow the switches. Couplings follow the joints in the run, which is a function of the length and the stock lengths you buy. That is why the accessory count is not a rate per square metre: a room with four switches and no sockets needs four boxes and four plates, whatever its floor area."
    },
    {
      "q": "Which conduit types can I estimate?",
      "a": "Surface, concealed and buried, each on its own line, because they are different products with different costs and different installation work. A surface run in a garage and a buried run to a gate light do not belong in one total. The estimator keeps them apart and applies your measured length to each, so a change of method shows in the order instead of disappearing into an average."
    },
    {
      "q": "How much cable goes inside a conduit?",
      "a": "The cable runs inside the conduit on the same path, so its length follows the conduit's length with the number of cores you specify for the circuit. Where a run carries three circuits, the cable is counted three times and the conduit sized for the group. A draw wire or spare capacity is a separate item you add deliberately, not an allowance folded into the cable, because a spare capacity that nobody records tends to be forgotten at second fix."
    }
  ],
