  "/faq/plumbing": [
    {
      "q": "How do I estimate plumbing materials for a building?",
      "a": "Measure the runs and count the fixtures. Supply, waste and drainage are measured separately because they take different pipe and different falls, and fittings are counted where those runs turn or branch. The estimate then returns pipe by diameter, fittings by type and the fixtures with their valves and traps. A plumbing estimate that starts from a fixture count and a rate per bathroom will be short on pipe and fittings every time."
    },
    {
      "q": "Does the plumbing estimate include a soakaway or septic tank?",
      "a": "No, it does not. A septic tank, soakaway or treatment plant is a separate design, sized from the occupancy and the ground, and it has its own material and excavation quantities. The estimator covers the pipe, fittings and fixtures inside the building. Where a drainage run leaves the building, measure it as a drainage run and price the tank or soakaway separately, so the two numbers stay checkable."
    },
    {
      "q": "Which pipe sizes should I choose?",
      "a": "From the design and the fixture demand, not from a table in a quantity tool. Supply sizes follow the flow and pressure available, waste sizes follow the fixtures discharging into them and the fall, and drainage follows both plus the local regulations. The estimator asks which size you are running and adds the length by size, so the order matches a design that has already been made."
    },
    {
      "q": "What does a plumbing estimate include?",
      "a": "Pipe measured by length, fittings counted, and the fixtures you schedule with the valves, traps and connections each one needs. Supply, waste and drainage stay on their own lines. Insulation, supports and fixings are counted where you specify them. It is a material estimate for the plumbing installation, and it deliberately stops at the fixtures rather than wandering into the rooms around them."
    },
    {
      "q": "How do I allow for pipe falls and bends?",
      "a": "Falls add length on drainage runs, since a pipe running at a gradient covers more distance than its horizontal projection, and bends add fittings at every change of direction. Measure the route along its actual line and count the bends the route needs. The estimator uses your measured route, and a route you have not measured is reported as missing instead of being closed with an assumption."
    }
  ],
