  "/faq/doors-windows": [
    {
      "q": "How do I estimate doors and windows for a building?",
      "a": "Count them from the door and window schedule, by type and size, then let the estimator add the frames, linings and ironmongery each unit needs. Two counts drive everything: how many units of each type, and what specification each type carries. A room list is not a schedule, because a bedroom door and a bathroom door are different products with different ironmongery, and a count that mixes them produces an order that has to be corrected on site."
    },
    {
      "q": "Are frames and hardware included?",
      "a": "Frames and architraves are added where your specification calls for them, and ironmongery is counted from the unit types: hinges by leaf, locksets and handles per door, stays and restrictors per window. What is not included is the installation side, which covers sealant, foam, packers, fixings and the labour to hang and adjust. Those are priced by the fitter, and folding them into a unit price hides the difference between a straightforward opening and an awkward one."
    },
    {
      "q": "Should I add a waste allowance to doors and windows?",
      "a": "No. A waste allowance exists because a material is cut from a longer length or a larger sheet, and a door is not cut from anything. Waste on doors and windows is really damage, mis measurement or a change of mind, and the way to handle that is a stated contingency rather than a percentage applied to every unit. The estimator returns whole units, which is what arrives on the lorry."
    },
    {
      "q": "What happens if I leave the door and window counts blank?",
      "a": "It reports the schedule as missing and leaves the line empty. A blank door count means the schedule has not been made yet, not that the building has no doors, and filling the gap with a count per room would produce an order that looks complete and is wrong. The page is designed so an unfinished design shows as unfinished, which is more useful than a number that has to be unpicked later."
    },
    {
      "q": "How are hinges and locksets handled?",
      "a": "Hinges are counted per leaf, so a pair of doors carries twice the hinges of a single leaf, and the specification decides whether that is two or three per leaf. Locksets, handles and closers are counted per door, with the type following the schedule, since a bathroom lock and a front door lock are different products. The estimator links all of this to the unit type, so changing a door's specification changes its ironmongery without a second pass."
    }
  ],
