  "/faq/quotes": [
    {
      "q": "What do the stages on a quote mean?",
      "a": "Open means sent and awaiting a decision. Viewed means the client has opened the link, which is a useful signal but not agreement. Accepted and declined are decisions, recorded by you or by the client. Expired means the validity date passed without an answer. Each state is recorded, so a quote that has been read is not treated the same as one that has not, and the totals on the page only claim what the records support."
    },
    {
      "q": "When does a quote expire?",
      "a": "On the validity date the quote carries, which you set when it is issued. A quote is an offer with a shelf life, and material prices move, so the date is part of the offer rather than an administrative detail. When it passes, the quote leaves the open list and appears as expired, and it can be revised and reissued at current prices. Reissuing creates a new quote, which is why the old one is left visible rather than deleted."
    },
    {
      "q": "Why are the totals shown per currency?",
      "a": "Because a quote in one currency cannot be added to a quote in another without an exchange rate, and inventing a rate would make the total a fiction. Each currency is summed on its own, so the pipeline shows what is owed in each. That matters in a market where a client pays in local currency and a supplier quotes in another, because the two totals answer different questions and a blended figure answers neither."
    },
    {
      "q": "How is the acceptance rate calculated?",
      "a": "From the quotes that reached a decision. Accepted divided by accepted plus declined, with open and expired quotes left out because they have not resolved either way. That is why the rate moves when a decision lands rather than when a quote is sent. Where no quote has resolved yet, the rate is reported as undetermined instead of as zero, because zero would read as failure when the truth is that there is no evidence yet."
    },
    {
      "q": "Which quotes should I chase?",
      "a": "The oldest open quotes first, and among those the ones that have been viewed without an answer. Age is the honest signal, because a quote that has been sitting for three weeks is closer to expiry than one sent on Monday. The pipeline orders them by age and marks the ones that have passed your chasing threshold. Which one to call first is a judgement about the client and the job, and the page gives you the facts to make it rather than the decision itself."
    }
  ],
