  "/faq/client-estimate": [
    {
      "q": "How is the total on a quote built?",
      "a": "From the priced lines. Each line carries a quantity from a calculator and a rate you set, and the total is their sum, not a rate applied to a floor area. Transport and tax appear as their own lines when they are configured, so the arithmetic is visible at every step. That structure is deliberate: a client who queries a total can be shown which line produced it, which is not possible with a single figure carrying an undisclosed loading."
    },
    {
      "q": "Does editing a quote recalculate the job?",
      "a": "A draft does, because a quote should follow the job. Change a dimension and the quantity changes, so the draft recalculates and the total reflects what is now being offered. An approved quote does not, because it is a record of an agreement rather than a working document. That split is the whole reason the system distinguishes the two states, and it is what allows a revision to be compared against the original."
    },
    {
      "q": "Why can I not edit an approved quote?",
      "a": "Because the client agreed to a specific document. Editing an approved quote in place would leave the copy they hold and the copy you hold describing different jobs, which is where disputes start. If the scope changes, the job is re measured and a new quote is issued with its own validity date, so the history shows what was offered, what was accepted and what changed. The approved original stays visible, which is the point of keeping it."
    },
    {
      "q": "What does the client see when I share a quote?",
      "a": "The same lines and the same total, in the order they appear, with the validity date the quote carries. There is no separate client view that rounds or hides a line, and no price that appears only on one side. If transport or tax was not included, the document shows the total without it, which is more honest than an inclusive figure that nobody can decompose when a client asks why the number moved."
    },
    {
      "q": "Are transport and tax added automatically?",
      "a": "Only where they are configured. Transport is a line you add when you charge it. Tax is not determined by the engine: where a rule applies to your market it appears with its basis stated, and where none applies the quote shows a total without tax and says so. That refusal is deliberate, because an invented percentage produces a number that looks final, is wrong for the jurisdiction, and is very hard to correct once a client has accepted it."
    }
  ],
