  "/faq/using-frelux": [
    {
      "q": "Are the FRELUX construction calculators free?",
      "a": "The calculators are free to use, and no account is needed to measure a quantity or read an answer page. An account is what carries the work: saved estimates, quotes with their states, and the client side of a quote. Measuring is free because a quantity should never be behind a paywall; keeping a pipeline of quotes and their history is the part that costs money to run."
    },
    {
      "q": "Do I need an account to use the calculators?",
      "a": "No. You can open any calculator, enter dimensions and read the result without one. An account is needed when you want to keep the estimate, send it as a quote, or come back to it later. The distinction is deliberate: a visitor checking how many blocks a wall needs should not have to register to find out, and a contractor running a pipeline needs the records an account provides."
    },
    {
      "q": "How accurate are FRELUX calculators?",
      "a": "The quantity is as accurate as the dimensions you enter, and the arithmetic on top of them is exact. Where the answer is less certain, the page says why: a coverage rate is a property of a product you select, a price comes from a market book or from you, and a tax line is left undetermined rather than assumed. So the honest answer is that the measurement is reliable, the inputs are yours, and the places where an assumption would otherwise sit are visible."
    },
    {
      "q": "Where do the prices in an estimate come from?",
      "a": "Either from the price book of the market you select, or from a figure you enter. Where a market has a verified book, the tool uses it and the line can be traced to it. Where it does not, the tool asks for the price rather than falling back to another country's number, because a labour or material rate imported from a different market is a fiction with a decimal point. The same rule applies to steel, where no default exists at all."
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does an estimate say tax is not determined?",
      "a": "Because applying a percentage that is not the rate for your jurisdiction would produce a confident wrong number, and a wrong number is worse than a stated gap. Where a tax rule is configured for your market, it appears as a line with its basis shown. Where none is configured, the estimate says tax is not determined. You can then add the rate that applies to you, and the total carries it as your figure rather than as the tool's assumption."
    },
    {
      "q": "How long does identity verification take?",
      "a": "An administrator normally reviews a submitted identity document the same working day. If no administrator is available, the request is approved automatically fifteen minutes after submission, so verification never waits on someone being at a desk, and the account changes status without you resubmitting."
    },
    {
      "q": "Why is my account still unverified after I uploaded my ID?",
      "a": "Because uploading a document does not grant pro status by itself. The document is held as a pending check while the account stays a client account and unverified, until an administrator approves it or the fifteen minute automatic approval runs and elevates the account."
    },
    {
      "q": "Which document can I upload to become a pro?",
      "a": "One government identity document in your own name: a Nigerian NIN of eleven digits, an international passport, a drivers licence or a voters card. The number must be readable and must match the name on the account, and anything that does not match is sent back for review."
    }
  ],
