  "/faq/masonry-calculator": [
    {
      "q": "Which wall types does the masonry tool cover?",
      "a": "Block walls, brick walls and thin joint systems, with the unit size and joint thickness following the type you select. Each has its own coverage per unit and its own mortar volume, so the tool does not blend them. Where a wall uses more than one type, measure each as its own wall and add the results, which keeps the block count and the brick count separate for ordering."
    },
    {
      "q": "How are openings handled?",
      "a": "Deducted by width and height with their quantity, so doors, windows and vents come out of the unit count rather than being added as an afterthought. An opening is an area, not a count, so a wide low window and a narrow tall one are handled by their dimensions. What is not deducted is the extra cutting and the lintel above, which are separate items you price on their own."
    },
    {
      "q": "Does it work in different countries and markets?",
      "a": "Yes. Cement packaging and mortar specifications come from the market you select, so a fifty kilogramme bag and a twenty five kilogramme bag are both handled, and the engine never falls back to one country's packaging when a market is set. Where the market's specification differs on joint thickness or mix, that is an input you can enter, so the same wall can be priced to a local practice without changing the tool."
    },
    {
      "q": "Are mortar and packaging included?",
      "a": "Both are included, and they follow the wall rather than being added per square metre. Mortar volume follows the wall type and the joint thickness, so a thin joint system uses a fraction of the mortar a ten millimetre joint does, and the cement follows the mix you specify. Cement is expressed in the bag size sold in your market, so the order is a number of bags rather than a weight to convert at the counter."
    },
    {
      "q": "Does it check the wall structurally?",
      "a": "No. It measures materials. Slenderness, lateral support, movement joints and reinforcement for a wall that is retaining or carrying load belong to an engineer, and a quantity tool that offered an opinion on them would be answering a question it cannot see the inputs for. Use the count to order blocks and the engineer's design to decide how the wall is built."
    }
  ],
