  "/faq/paving-calculator": [
    {
      "q": "How are pavers counted?",
      "a": "Area divided by the area of one paver including its joint, then rounded up, with a cutting allowance for the pattern. A two hundred by one hundred paver at a ten millimetre joint covers about two hundred and thirty one square centimetres of finished surface, so forty eight square metres needs roughly two thousand two hundred. The count comes back in whole pavers, and the pattern adds to it, because herringbone cuts differently from a stack bond."
    },
    {
      "q": "Does the laying pattern change the quantity?",
      "a": "Yes, through the cutting allowance. A stack bond in a rectangular area loses very little to cuts, a running bond loses more at the edges, and a herringbone or basket weave loses more again because the offcuts rarely fit anywhere. The calculator applies the allowance for the pattern you select, so the same area in two patterns produces two different paver counts, which matches what a paver layer would order."
    },
    {
      "q": "Are bedding sand and subbase included?",
      "a": "Both are calculated from the area and the depths you enter, not assumed. Bedding sand is a thickness over the whole area, and subbase is a greater thickness that is compacted to a finished level, so it is ordered as a loose volume or a tonnage. Because the depths are yours, a garden path at a hundred millimetres of subbase and a driveway at two hundred are priced as the different jobs they are."
    },
    {
      "q": "Are these depths design values?",
      "a": "No, they are inputs. Subbase and bedding depths follow from the traffic, the ground and the drainage, and they are the difference between a pavement that lasts and one that ruts in the first wet season. The calculator asks for the depths your specification calls for and shows the volumes they imply, so you can see the material cost of a specification change before you commit to it."
    },
    {
      "q": "Is edge restraint included?",
      "a": "Edge restraint is calculated from the perimeter, less the openings you enter, and it is included as a line because it is bought by the metre and often forgotten. A pavement that is not restrained at its edges spreads, whatever the subbase. The perimeter also gives you the length of any channel or kerb you are adding, which is why the takeoff asks for the plan rather than only the area."
    }
  ],
