  "/faq/plastering-calculator": [
    {
      "q": "What does the plastering tool distinguish?",
      "a": "Internal plaster and external render, and within those the difference between a gypsum finish and a cement based one. They differ in mix, thickness and preparation, so the calculator asks which you are applying before it calculates anything. Where a wall takes a render base coat and a plaster skim, measure the two as their own surfaces, because the products and the thicknesses are not interchangeable."
    },
    {
      "q": "How is the sand and cement worked out?",
      "a": "From the area and the thickness, which gives a volume, then from the mix you specify. A one to four mix splits that volume into one part cement and four of sand, and the cement is then converted to the bag size sold in your market. A bagged product instead asks for its coverage, because a manufactured plaster states how many square metres a bag will cover at a stated thickness, which is a more direct route than a mix."
    },
    {
      "q": "Does thickness really change the quantity that much?",
      "a": "Yes, and more than most people expect, because material follows volume. Doubling the thickness doubles the sand and cement on the same wall, and a two coat render at eighteen millimetres total uses well over twice the material of a single ten millimetre coat. That is why the thickness is an input and why the page shows the volume: a specification change that looks like a detail on a drawing is a lorry load of sand."
    },
    {
      "q": "Are beads, scrim and bonding agents included?",
      "a": "Beads, scrim and bonding agents are counted where the specification calls for them. Beads follow the corners, reveals and stops you enter, measured as lengths, since a stop bead at a ceiling line is a metre of bead rather than an area. Scrim follows the joints and the changes of material. Bonding agent follows the area you treat, at its stated coverage, and it is the item most often left out of a plaster estimate."
    },
    {
      "q": "Does it allow for surface preparation?",
      "a": "Only where you enter it. Surface preparation is a site judgement: a dusty wall needs a bonding agent, a smooth concrete wall needs a key, and a painted wall may need more than either. The calculator asks for the area you are treating and adds the product at its coverage, so the preparation is priced as its own line rather than folded into a trowel rate that assumes a perfect substrate."
    }
  ],
