  "/faq/reinforcement": [
    {
      "q": "What does the reinforcement tool need from me?",
      "a": "A bar schedule. For each bar mark the tool needs a diameter, a length and a number of bars, which is exactly what a bending schedule already states. From those three it calculates the weight using the unit mass of that bar size, sums the marks by diameter, and returns a total in kilogrammes. If you have a drawing without a schedule, the schedule has to be made first, because the weight cannot be inferred from the concrete."
    },
    {
      "q": "How is the weight calculated?",
      "a": "Diameter gives the unit mass, and the mass multiplied by the length gives the weight of one bar, and that multiplied by the count gives the bar mark's total. A sixteen millimetre bar is about one point five eight kilogrammes for every metre, so a seven metre bar is eleven kilogrammes. The unit masses are physical properties of the bar, not market prices, so the arithmetic is the same wherever the steel is being bought."
    },
    {
      "q": "Which diameters are supported?",
      "a": "The common diameters from six millimetres to thirty two, which covers most building work. Ranges beyond that depend on the supplier, and where a bar size is not in the list it can still be entered by its unit mass. Grade matters for strength but not for weight, so a schedule in a higher grade weighs the same as one in a lower grade, which is why the tool asks for diameter rather than grade."
    },
    {
      "q": "Is binding wire included?",
      "a": "Binding wire is not included, and neither are spacers, chairs or tying labour. They are real costs and they are usually priced per tonne of reinforcement or as a separate sundries line. The estimate covers the bar weight from the schedule, which is what the fabricator quotes and what arrives on the lorry. Adding a wire percentage would be inventing a figure whose basis nobody could check later."
    },
    {
      "q": "Does it design the reinforcement?",
      "a": "No. It measures the bars you specify. How much steel an element needs, where laps fall, how bars are anchored and curtailed are design decisions that come from the structural engineer's calculations. The tool's value is that it turns a finished schedule into a weight, a cost and an order, and it is deliberately incapable of deciding the schedule itself."
    }
  ],
