  "/faq/waterproofing": [
    {
      "q": "Which waterproofing does it cover?",
      "a": "Liquid applied coatings, sheet membranes and cementitious systems, each with its own coverage and detailing. The one you choose follows from the substrate, the water pressure and what will be laid over it, and that is a specification decision. The calculator asks which family you are using, because a sheet membrane is ordered in square metres with laps and a liquid is ordered in kilogrammes per coat."
    },
    {
      "q": "How are coats and coverage handled?",
      "a": "Coverage per coat from the product, multiplied by the area, then multiplied by the number of coats you enter. Both figures are shown, because the per coat number is what tells you whether a tin is enough for one pass and the total is what you order. Coats are never assumed: a two coat system specified by a manufacturer and applied as one coat is the most common way a waterproofing job fails."
    },
    {
      "q": "What about membranes and tape?",
      "a": "Both are counted from the details you list rather than from the area. Tape goes at the floor to wall junctions, the internal and external corners and the pipe penetrations, which means a small bathroom can need more tape than its area suggests. Sheet membranes also need their joints taped. Reinforcement fabric goes where movement is expected, and the calculator counts it from the runs you describe."
    },
    {
      "q": "What happens if I leave an area blank?",
      "a": "A blank area is reported as missing and left out of the total, so an incomplete scope is visible rather than invisible. Treating a blank as zero is how a tanking estimate comes to look complete while missing a wall. The same applies to a detail with no length, which is reported rather than assumed to be a metre."
    },
    {
      "q": "Does it guarantee the detail is watertight?",
      "a": "No. It calculates materials for a system you have specified. Whether the detail is watertight depends on the substrate, the preparation, the number of coats actually applied, the laps and the workmanship, and none of those can be verified from a quantity. What the estimate does is make sure the material to do the job properly is on site, which is the part that can be arranged in advance."
    }
  ],
