  "/faq/structural-calculator": [
    {
      "q": "What does the structural takeoff measure?",
      "a": "It measures concrete and reinforcement for the elements you describe. Each slab, beam, column, footing or wall is entered with its own dimensions, and the quantities roll up by material. Reinforcement is counted by bar diameter, length and number, then converted to mass using the unit weight of that bar size, which is how a steel order is placed. What it does not do is decide the dimensions: those come from the structural design, and the schedule assumes they are already fixed."
    },
    {
      "q": "Where do the dimensions come from?",
      "a": "From you, and from the drawing you are reading. The length, breadth and depth of each element are entered as they appear on the design, and the bar schedule supplies the diameters and lengths for reinforcement. The tool does not scale a drawing or infer a section from a span, because a takeoff built on inference is a takeoff that cannot be checked. Where a dimension is missing, it is reported as missing so the gap is closed on the drawing before anything is ordered."
    },
    {
      "q": "Is this a structural design?",
      "a": "No. It is a material schedule, which is a different document from a design. Sizing members, calculating loads, checking deflection and detailing reinforcement are engineering work, and the tool assumes that work is done. The value it adds is turning a finished design into quantities and an order that can be placed, checked and audited, which is where most of the arithmetic and nearly all of the clerical error live."
    },
    {
      "q": "How are the concrete ingredients split?",
      "a": "From the mix you specify, in proportions by volume, converted to the bag size sold in your market. A one to one and a half to three mix and a one to two to four mix give noticeably different cement content for the same concrete volume, so the mix is an input rather than a default. Sand and aggregate are then expressed by volume or by tonne, and cement by bag, because that is how each is bought at a merchant in most markets."
    },
    {
      "q": "Can I mix slabs, beams, columns and footings?",
      "a": "Yes, and they should be, because a frame is placed and poured in sequence rather than in one operation. Each element keeps its own line with its own dimensions and quantity, and the materials roll up across the whole set. That way a slab can be ordered for one pour and columns for another, while the totals still tell you what the whole frame needs. Mixing everything into a single volume loses the sequence and makes a partial pour impossible to price."
    }
  ],
