  "/faq/concrete-takeoff-calculator": [
    {
      "q": "How is slab concrete calculated?",
      "a": "Area multiplied by thickness, plus any thickenings and poured edges you describe, plus a waste allowance. The takeoff does that from the dimensions you enter and shows the volume before and after waste, because a checking engineer wants to see both. The volume then converts into cement, sand and aggregate at the mix you select, in the units your market sells, so the order is in bags and tonnes rather than cubic metres of concrete that nobody delivers."
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does the thickness matter so much?",
      "a": "Because volume is area times depth, so the depth multiplies the entire order rather than adding to it. Going from a hundred to a hundred and fifty millimetres on the same eighty square metre slab adds four cubic metres of concrete, and with it a tonne and a half of cement and several tonnes of aggregate. That is why a slab depth is a design decision with a material consequence, and why the page will not assume one for you."
    },
    {
      "q": "Does it allow for a poured edge or beam thickening?",
      "a": "Yes, where you enter them. A thickening under a load bearing wall or a poured edge at the perimeter is a run with a width and a depth, and the takeoff adds its volume to the slab rather than ignoring it. Most slab estimates that come out short are short on exactly these details, because the area was measured and the edges were not."
    },
    {
      "q": "Is reinforcement included?",
      "a": "No. Reinforcement is measured from a bar schedule, by diameter, length and count, which is a different calculation with a different unit. A percentage of concrete volume is a rule of thumb that produces a figure nobody can check against the design. The takeoff says plainly that the steel is separate, and the reinforcement tool measures it properly."
    },
    {
      "q": "Does it check the slab structurally?",
      "a": "No. It measures materials for a slab whose thickness and reinforcement have been designed. Span to depth ratios, loading, restraint and crack control all belong to a structural engineer, and a slab that is under designed does not fail politely. The takeoff's job is to turn a decided design into concrete, sand and aggregate that can be ordered and poured."
    }
  ],
