  "/faq/earthworks-cut-fill": [
    {
      "q": "How are cut and fill volumes calculated?",
      "a": "Area multiplied by depth for each excavation and each area to be raised, then adjusted for how soil behaves when it is moved. The calculator takes each area and depth you enter, sums the cut and the fill separately, and applies bulking to the former and compaction to the latter. What it will not do is assume a factor: both are inputs, because they vary with the soil and the method."
    },
    {
      "q": "Why separate cut from fill?",
      "a": "Because excavated soil occupies more space than it did in the ground, and compacted fill occupies less. Adding the two raw volumes and subtracting one from the other produces a balance that no lorry can achieve. Keeping them separate lets each be adjusted by its own factor, and the difference between them becomes a real quantity of soil to import or remove, which is the number the haulage is priced on."
    },
    {
      "q": "What are the bulking and compaction factors for?",
      "a": "Bulking accounts for the increase in volume when soil is disturbed, which is why a cubic metre of cut becomes more than a cubic metre in a lorry. Compaction accounts for the reduction when fill is placed in layers and rolled, which is why a cubic metre of fill needs more than a cubic metre of loose material to achieve it. Both are properties of the soil and the method, so both are yours to enter rather than defaults the tool invents."
    },
    {
      "q": "Can I price the haulage?",
      "a": "Haulage is calculated from the volume to be moved and the capacity of the vehicle you enter, in loads. What is not included is the distance, the rate per load or the time, because those are commercial terms rather than quantities. You get the number of loads, which is the figure a haulier quotes against, and the surplus or deficit that decides whether they are taking soil away or bringing it in."
    },
    {
      "q": "Is this geotechnical advice?",
      "a": "No. It is a volume calculation for a site whose levels have been designed. Soil classification, bearing capacity, slope stability, groundwater and contamination are geotechnical questions, and the factors you enter are assumptions that a geotechnical assessment would confirm or correct. Use the takeoff to price the movement you have designed, and use a geotechnical engineer to decide what can be moved and where it can go."
    }
  ],
