  "/faq/roof-framing-calculator": [
    {
      "q": "What does the roof framing tool measure?",
      "a": "Members by length and by volume: rafters, ridge, hips, valleys, purlins and battens, each from the pitch, the span and the spacing you enter. Board feet and linear metres are both returned because merchants price timber differently across markets. What it does not measure is the covering, the fixings or the trusses, since those follow their own rules and their own schedules."
    },
    {
      "q": "How does pitch change the quantity?",
      "a": "Through the slope. A roof at thirty degrees has a sloped area of the plan divided by the cosine of the pitch, so a hundred square metres of plan becomes about a hundred and fifteen of roof. Rafters get longer as the pitch rises for the same span, and their count per unit of plan area rises with the slope. Both effects are in the arithmetic, which is why pitch is the first input rather than a detail."
    },
    {
      "q": "Why board feet as well as linear length?",
      "a": "Because timber is sold and priced both ways. A merchant may quote a length and a rate per linear metre, while a mill or a specification may price by board feet, which is a volume measure of the nominal section. Returning both from one schedule means the order does not have to be converted by hand, and a conversion done at the counter is where the two figures usually drift apart."
    },
    {
      "q": "Does it size the members?",
      "a": "No. Member sizing follows from the span, the load, the spacing and the grade of timber, and it comes from span tables or an engineer's design. The takeoff measures the members a design calls for, at the sections you specify. Where a span exceeds the tables, that is a design question with a real answer, and it is not one a quantity tool should be inventing."
    },
    {
      "q": "Are roof coverings included?",
      "a": "No. The covering is measured by the roofing tool, which converts the sloped area into sheets or tiles at their cover width and lap. The framing takeoff covers the structure that carries the covering. Keeping them apart matters because a change of covering changes the battens and the spacing but not the rafters, and a single blended estimate would obscure that."
    }
  ],
