  "/faq/temporary-works-calculator": [
    {
      "q": "What does the temporary works tool take off?",
      "a": "Contact area, and then the materials that area implies: ply in sheets, primary and secondary bearers, props and fittings, with a reuse allowance. It measures the surfaces the concrete touches, because that is what formwork is. Where an element has a void, a chamfer or a rebate, those are extra runs you describe, since they add contact area the main dimensions do not capture."
    },
    {
      "q": "How are reuse cycles treated?",
      "a": "As a divisor on the purchased material. Ply used for one pour is consumed by that pour; ply used six times spreads its cost across six. The takeoff divides the area by the cycles you enter so the cost per pour is visible, and it leaves the cycles to you because they depend on the care taken striking, the surface finish required and how many identical pours there are."
    },
    {
      "q": "Why measure contact area instead of volume?",
      "a": "Because formwork is a surface and concrete is a volume. The formwork that a beam needs is its soffit and its sides, which are areas, not its cubic metres. Measuring by volume would give the wrong order in both directions: a deep narrow beam and a shallow wide one of the same volume have very different contact areas."
    },
    {
      "q": "Does it check the falsework?",
      "a": "No. Prop loads, bearer sizes, the grid, the foundation under the props and the striking sequence are design decisions, and falsework failures are among the most serious on site. The takeoff measures the materials for a falsework design that has been made. It does not check whether the design holds, and it does not replace the temporary works coordinator."
    },
    {
      "q": "Are scaffold and access included?",
      "a": "No. Scaffolding, edge protection, ladders and access platforms are separate systems with their own hire and their own design. The takeoff covers formwork and falsework materials, which is the part that is consumed or amortised by the concrete. Access is usually hired by duration and priced separately, and mixing the two produces a figure that answers neither question."
    }
  ],
