  "/faq/septic-sizing-calculator": [
    {
      "q": "What decides the septic tank size?",
      "a": "The daily flow and the retention period, and the sludge and scum storage the standard requires on top of that. Flow follows the occupancy and the fixtures, and it is the number that matters, because a tank sized by bedroom count is sized by a proxy for people who may not be there. Enter the flow, choose the retention, and the working volume follows, with the materials for it."
    },
    {
      "q": "Why do I have to enter the flow rate myself?",
      "a": "Because a flow rate derived from a bedroom count is an assumption about occupancy, and the consequences of getting it wrong are unpleasant and expensive to fix. A house with six occupants and a house of the same size with two produce very different loads, and a guest house is different again. Since you know the intended use and the tool does not, the flow is your input, and the calculation refuses to proceed without it."
    },
    {
      "q": "How is the soakaway sized?",
      "a": "From the daily flow and the infiltration rate of the ground, which comes from a percolation test. The soakaway has to absorb what the tank discharges, so its area follows the flow divided by the rate at which the ground accepts it, and a clay soil needs many times the area of a sandy one for the same house. The materials then follow the area: pipe, granular fill and geotextile for the trench or the pit you describe."
    },
    {
      "q": "Does it issue a permit or environmental approval?",
      "a": "No. It sizes a system and counts its materials. Permits, discharge consent, setback distances, groundwater protection and the treatment standard are administered locally, and the numbers here are inputs to that process rather than a substitute for it. Where a site is sensitive, the design may need to be different from the standard solution the calculator assumes."
    },
    {
      "q": "Can I work in gallons and feet?",
      "a": "Yes. Enter volumes in litres or gallons and dimensions in metres or feet, and the calculation stays in the unit you chose rather than converting mid way. That matters for a tank specified in gallons by a supplier and a soakaway area measured in square metres on site, which is a common combination and a common source of mistakes when the conversion happens twice."
    }
  ],
  "/septic-sizing-calculator": [
    {
      "q": "What decides the septic tank size?",
      "a": "The daily flow and the retention period, and the sludge and scum storage the standard requires on top of that. Flow follows the occupancy and the fixtures, and it is the number that matters, because a tank sized by bedroom count is sized by a proxy for people who may not be there. Enter the flow, choose the retention, and the working volume follows, with the materials for it."
    },
    {
      "q": "Why do I have to enter the flow rate myself?",
      "a": "Because a flow rate derived from a bedroom count is an assumption about occupancy, and the consequences of getting it wrong are unpleasant and expensive to fix. A house with six occupants and a house of the same size with two produce very different loads, and a guest house is different again. Since you know the intended use and the tool does not, the flow is your input, and the calculation refuses to proceed without it."
    },
    {
      "q": "How is the soakaway sized?",
      "a": "From the daily flow and the infiltration rate of the ground, which comes from a percolation test. The soakaway has to absorb what the tank discharges, so its area follows the flow divided by the rate at which the ground accepts it, and a clay soil needs many times the area of a sandy one for the same house. The materials then follow the area: pipe, granular fill and geotextile for the trench or the pit you describe."
    },
    {
      "q": "Does it issue a permit or environmental approval?",
      "a": "No. It sizes a system and counts its materials. Permits, discharge consent, setback distances, groundwater protection and the treatment standard are administered locally, and the numbers here are inputs to that process rather than a substitute for it. Where a site is sensitive, the design may need to be different from the standard solution the calculator assumes."
    },
    {
      "q": "Can I work in gallons and feet?",
      "a": "Yes. Enter volumes in litres or gallons and dimensions in metres or feet, and the calculation stays in the unit you chose rather than converting mid way. That matters for a tank specified in gallons by a supplier and a soakaway area measured in square metres on site, which is a common combination and a common source of mistakes when the conversion happens twice."
    }
  ],
  "/faq/insulation-calculator": [
    {
      "q": "What does the insulation takeoff measure?",
      "a": "Insulation area by element, converted into packs at the product's coverage, plus vapour barriers, membranes and fixings. It handles more than one layer, because a specification often calls for two: one between the studs and one across them. What it does not do is judge the specification, since the thickness and the product follow from a thermal calculation rather than from a quantity takeoff."
    },
    {
      "q": "Does it calculate thermal performance?",
      "a": "No. Thermal performance comes from the whole build up: the insulation, the structure, the cavity, the surface resistances and the thermal bridges, and it is calculated with those in front of you. The takeoff measures material. Reporting a U value from an insulation area alone would produce a figure that looks authoritative and ignores the timber studs every six hundred millimetres, which is where most of the heat actually goes."
    },
    {
      "q": "How do layers affect the order?",
      "a": "Each layer is counted as its own material with its own coverage, so a hundred millimetre layer between the studs and a second layer across them are two orders rather than one doubled figure. That matters because the second layer is frequently a different product at a different thickness, and because the fixings and the tapes change when a layer is added."
    },
    {
      "q": "Is pack rounding applied?",
      "a": "Yes. Rolls and packs cover a stated area at a stated thickness, so the takeoff divides and rounds up to whole packs, which is what a merchant sells. Where the coverage differs by thickness, as it does with mineral wool, the coverage for the thickness you selected is used rather than a figure for a different product."
    },
    {
      "q": "What about vapour barriers and fixings?",
      "a": "Vapour barriers, membranes, tapes and fixings are counted where the specification calls for them, each at its own coverage. A vapour barrier is measured over the area it covers with the laps you allow, and fixings follow the spacing you enter. They are small items that decide whether the insulation performs as designed, which is why they are lines rather than an allowance."
    }
  ],
