  "/faq/solar-pv-estimator": [
    {
      "q": "How does the solar estimator work?",
      "a": "As a component takeoff. You describe the array size and the equipment, and it returns panels, inverters, isolators, mounting, cable and protection at the quantities that configuration needs, priced from the market book or from figures you supply. It does not decide the size of the system, because that follows from the load and the roof, and it does not forecast production, because that follows from the site."
    },
    {
      "q": "Where do panel and component figures come from?",
      "a": "Panel and inverter counts follow from the array size and the equipment you specify, mounting follows the panel count and the rail system, and cable follows the measured runs. Every one of those is arithmetic once the design is fixed. Where a component's coverage is a property of the product, such as how many clamps a rail system needs per panel, that comes from the product record rather than from a guess."
    },
    {
      "q": "What if a component has no price?",
      "a": "It is reported as a gap. A component carried at zero would make the quote look complete and would produce a total that cannot be delivered, and on a solar job the unpriced item is often the battery or the mounting system, which is not a rounding error. The page shows the gap so it can be closed with a real figure before the quote goes out."
    },
    {
      "q": "Does it design the array?",
      "a": "No. Array size, string length, inverter matching, protection and earthing are design decisions that follow from the load profile, the roof and the regulations where you are building. What the tool does is take a decided design and return the components and their quantities, which is the part that can be counted and priced before anything is ordered."
    },
    {
      "q": "Are sun hours and losses assumed?",
      "a": "No. Sun hours, tilt, azimuth, shading and system losses are all site specific, and a default figure would produce a yield estimate that looks precise and is wrong for most locations. Where you supply a figure, the page uses it and the assumption is visible. Where you do not, the estimate covers materials and leaves production to a proper yield calculation."
    }
  ],
