  "/faq/generator": [
    {
      "q": "Does the tool size my generator?",
      "a": "No. Generator sizing follows from the connected load, the starting current of the largest motor, the diversity you expect and whether the set takes the whole building or a protected subset, and it is calculated by an electrical engineer rather than by a quantity tool. The tool takes a size you have decided and returns the equipment and connections that size requires, which is the part a supplier and an installer both need."
    },
    {
      "q": "What does it actually take off?",
      "a": "The set, the changeover, the protection, the cable by size, the earthing materials, the fuel storage and any containment for the runs. Each is counted from your specification and your measured routes rather than from a rule per kilowatt. That matters because two installations of the same capacity can differ by a hundred metres of cable depending on where the set sits and where it connects."
    },
    {
      "q": "Why is a blank cable run reported as missing?",
      "a": "Because a generator is often the furthest thing from the board that it feeds, so its cable run is the longest on the site and the easiest to underestimate. A per metre allowance would be wrong in both directions, and the error lands on the most expensive cable in the building. Measuring the route and entering it keeps the order tied to the actual layout."
    },
    {
      "q": "Are fuel and consumption covered?",
      "a": "Fuel is not included as a cost. The tool counts storage capacity, which is a capital item, but consumption depends on the load profile and the set's specific consumption, and running cost depends on the fuel price where you are. Those are operating figures that belong in a different calculation from an installation takeoff."
    },
    {
      "q": "Is the installation checked?",
      "a": "No. Protection coordination, earthing design, changeover arrangements and the enclosure are all installation design, and the local wiring regulations govern them. The takeoff counts what the design calls for, so the installation can be priced and the materials ordered. Certification of the completed installation is a separate act by a competent person, and no quantity tool can stand in for it."
    }
  ],
