  "/faq/heat-comfort": [
    {
      "q": "What does the heat and comfort tool compare?",
      "a": "Two surface specifications. Each is described by its solar reflectance, its emittance and the product it came from, and the tool reports what those properties mean for the heat absorbed over the area you enter. It is a like for like comparison, so the value is in showing a client or a specifier the measured difference between a dark finish and a reflective one on the same building."
    },
    {
      "q": "Where do reflectance figures come from?",
      "a": "From the product data, and the page shows where each figure came from so it can be checked. Reflectance and emittance are measured properties with standard test methods, and manufacturers publish them. Where a product has no published value, the tool reports the gap rather than assigning a typical figure, because a comparison built on an assumed property proves nothing."
    },
    {
      "q": "Does it predict indoor temperature?",
      "a": "No. Indoor temperature depends on the whole building: the walls, the glazing, the shading, the ventilation, the thermal mass and how people use it. A surface comparison isolates one variable, which is what makes it useful and what makes it insufficient on its own. If you need a temperature prediction, that is a building simulation with the whole envelope in it."
    },
    {
      "q": "Why compare two surfaces instead of one?",
      "a": "Because a single surface tells you nothing. The point of the tool is the difference between two options on the same building, which is the decision actually being made when a roof colour or a coating is chosen. Comparing one surface with nothing would produce a number without a reference point, and the specification decision would still be made on impression."
    },
    {
      "q": "Does it qualify for any green certification?",
      "a": "No. Certification and rating schemes assess the whole building against a published standard, with modelling, verification and a assessor involved. A property comparison supports a specification choice, and it may be one input into a submission, but it does not qualify anything by itself. Claiming otherwise would be claiming a credential the tool cannot produce."
    }
  ],
