  "/faq/labour-estimator": [
    {
      "q": "How are worker days calculated?",
      "a": "By dividing each measured quantity by the output rate for that operation, then adjusting for the efficiency factor you set. Six hundred square metres of blockwork at twelve square metres per worker day is fifty days of work, and at an efficiency of zero point eight five it is fifty nine. The tool does that for each operation, sums by trade, and shows the crew size implied by the duration you want."
    },
    {
      "q": "Where does the site efficiency loss sit?",
      "a": "As a multiplier on the calculated days, applied explicitly so it can be seen and changed. Site conditions, access, weather and the learning curve all reduce output, and the factor is where that reduction lives. A factor of one assumes a textbook site, which few are. The value sits in the open rather than inside a rate, so a project manager can disagree with it specifically instead of arguing about the whole labour line."
    },
    {
      "q": "Why are durations whole days?",
      "a": "Because a crew is booked for days, not for fractions of one. Rounding at the operation level rather than at the total is deliberate: three operations of two and a half days are three crews for three days each in practice, not seven and a half days that can be squeezed. Rounding also makes the labour line slightly conservative, which is the right direction for an estimate that has to be delivered against."
    },
    {
      "q": "What if a productivity rate is missing?",
      "a": "It is reported as missing and left out of the total, so the labour estimate shows where its own coverage stops. A default rate would produce a duration with nothing behind it, and labour durations feed directly into a programme that other trades are booked against. Where you do not know a rate, the honest move is to measure it on a similar job or to ask a foreman, and then to enter it."
    },
    {
      "q": "Does it produce a full programme?",
      "a": "No. It converts quantities into worker days and shows the crew implied. A programme needs logic: which operations precede which, how many crews can work at once, what the critical path is and where the float sits. That is a separate exercise with its own tool, and the labour hours from this page are one of its inputs rather than its output."
    }
  ],
