  "/faq/margin-calculator": [
    {
      "q": "What is the difference between margin and markup?",
      "a": "Markup is a percentage added to cost. Margin is a percentage of the selling price. On a cost of a hundred, a twenty percent markup gives a price of a hundred and twenty and a margin of sixteen point seven percent, because the profit is twenty on a price of a hundred and twenty. The calculator shows both from one input, so the number being quoted is unambiguous."
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does the basis matter so much?",
      "a": "Because the two bases produce different prices from the same intention. A contractor who says they want twenty percent and applies it as markup is pricing at sixteen point seven percent of the price, which may be below the margin the business needs once overheads are recovered. Getting the basis wrong on a large job moves the price by thousands, and it is invisible unless both figures are shown."
    },
    {
      "q": "Is tax added automatically?",
      "a": "No. Tax is reported as its own line where a rule applies to your market, and as undetermined where none does. Folding an assumed percentage into a selling price produces a figure that looks final, is wrong for the jurisdiction and is very hard to unpick once quoted. Where you need to show tax, add the rate that applies to you and it is carried as your figure."
    },
    {
      "q": "Can I work backwards from a target price?",
      "a": "Yes. Enter a target margin and the calculator returns the price that achieves it, which is the direction most pricing decisions actually take. It is the same arithmetic rearranged, and it is more useful than forward pricing when a business knows the margin it needs to survive and is deciding whether a job is worth taking at all."
    },
    {
      "q": "Does it include overheads?",
      "a": "Overheads are kept separate from profit. Recovery is a different calculation, usually a rate per unit of turnover or per project, and mixing it into a margin hides whether the business is covering its fixed costs. The calculator shows cost, overhead recovery, profit and price as distinct lines, so a job that looks profitable at the margin line can still be seen to be thin once overheads are counted."
    }
  ],
