  "/faq/cash-flow-timeline": [
    {
      "q": "How are payment stages built?",
      "a": "From the percentages you set against milestones or dates in the programme, applied to the contract value. Each stage becomes a payment due in a period, and its value follows from the percentage. Where a stage is tied to an event, such as roof level, its date comes from the programme rather than from a guess, which is why the two tools belong together."
    },
    {
      "q": "What happens if the percentages do not total one hundred?",
      "a": "It reports the remainder. A schedule of twenty, thirty and forty leaves ten percent unallocated, and the tool shows that as a gap rather than quietly scaling the others up. On a hundred and twenty thousand contract that gap is twelve thousand, which is the kind of money that turns into a dispute at the end of a job, so it is worth seeing while the contract is still being drafted."
    },
    {
      "q": "Can I model a retention?",
      "a": "Yes, as a percentage held from each payment and released at the point you specify. It shows the cumulative amount held through the job and the amount released at the end, which matters because retention is money earned, invoiced and unavailable for months. A job that looks profitable on the margin line can still fail on the cash it has tied up in retention."
    },
    {
      "q": "Does it track actual payments?",
      "a": "No. Actual payments are a ledger, and this is a forecast. What it shows is what should be due when, based on the stages you have agreed. Reconciling that with what has actually arrived is bookkeeping, and a forecast that pretends to be a ledger is worse than either on its own."
    },
    {
      "q": "Why phase payments at all?",
      "a": "Because a single payment at the end means the contractor funds the whole job. Staging moves money closer to the work, which reduces the funding requirement and spreads the risk. The tool shows what each staging arrangement does to that requirement, so a negotiation about payment terms can be had with numbers rather than with instinct."
    }
  ],
