  "/faq/project-timeline": [
    {
      "q": "What does the timeline build from?",
      "a": "Operations, their durations and their dependencies. Durations come from quantities divided by productivity, or from figures you enter where you know better. Dependencies encode what must follow what, which is the part that turns a list into a programme. The output is a sequence with a critical path and float on the operations that have any."
    },
    {
      "q": "Where do durations come from?",
      "a": "From the quantities and the productivity rates, so a programme is built on measurement rather than optimism. Where you supply a duration directly, it is used and the derivation is not needed. Where neither exists, the operation is reported as unplaced, because a bar on a chart with no basis is why programmes slip on the first week and are quietly abandoned by the fourth."
    },
    {
      "q": "Can I see the critical path?",
      "a": "Yes, on the operations that carry no float. The critical path is the chain whose duration equals the project duration, so a delay anywhere on it moves completion. The tool identifies that chain and shows the float on everything else, which is the information needed to decide whether a delay matters or can be absorbed."
    },
    {
      "q": "Does it track progress?",
      "a": "No. Tracking progress means recording what has actually been done, against when, and recalculating from there, and that is a different exercise from building a programme. The timeline is a plan: it says what should happen in what order and how long it should take. Comparing that with reality is how a project is managed, and the tool does not pretend to do it."
    },
    {
      "q": "What about weather and access?",
      "a": "They are allowances you enter. Weather, access restrictions and non working days vary by site, season and locality, and a default would be wrong somewhere. The tool applies the allowances you set to the operations affected, so a rainy season extension is visible in the completion date instead of emerging as a surprise in month three."
    }
  ],
