  "/faq/construction-sequence": [
    {
      "q": "What is the construction sequence for?",
      "a": "It sets out the order work has to happen in and which operations can overlap. A sequence is what a programme is built on: without it, a bar chart is a wish. It also makes hold points explicit, which matters because concrete poured over an uninspected footing cannot be checked afterwards. The value is in the dependencies being stated rather than remembered by whoever has been on site longest."
    },
    {
      "q": "Is the sequence fixed?",
      "a": "The structural dependencies are fixed: you cannot build a wall before its footing, or a ceiling before the roof is covered. The overlaps are not fixed, and they depend on the building, the access and how many crews you have. The tool marks which is which, so a decision to overlap is a decision rather than a drift."
    },
    {
      "q": "How does it relate to the timeline?",
      "a": "The sequence is the logic and the timeline is the calendar. The sequence says what must follow what; the timeline turns that into durations and dates, and identifies the critical path. Building a programme from a sequence is straightforward, while building one without a sequence produces dates that cannot be delivered because the order is wrong."
    },
    {
      "q": "Does it include inspections?",
      "a": "Inspections and hold points are in the sequence at the points where work gets covered: footings before concrete, first fix before plaster, drainage before backfill. They are not an afterthought, because an inspection that cannot be done is a defect that cannot be seen. What the tool does not do is decide which inspections your authority requires, which is a local question."
    },
    {
      "q": "Who uses it?",
      "a": "Site managers, foremen and anyone pricing work from a programme. A subcontractor can see where their operation sits and what has to be finished before they can start, which is the conversation that otherwise happens on the morning they arrive. A client can see what stage their money is buying, which is why sequence and payment stages are usually read together."
    }
  ],
