  "/faq/maintenance-planner": [
    {
      "q": "Where do service lives come from?",
      "a": "From published service lives attached to the components you list, and each is editable because exposure changes it. A roof covering has a nominal life, a paint system has a shorter one, a serviceable plant item has an interval rather than a life. The figures are starting points with a source, not predictions about a particular building."
    },
    {
      "q": "What does the planner produce?",
      "a": "A schedule of tasks by year, with their costs, the annual total across the horizon, and the peaks where work coincides. It is what a sinking fund is calculated from and what a building owner needs to know before a lease is signed. The output is a plan, so the work can be budgeted and procured rather than discovered."
    },
    {
      "q": "Can I change an interval?",
      "a": "Yes, and often you should. A coastal location shortens the life of external coatings, a harsh sun shortens the life of sealants, and a sheltered interior lengthens both. The interval you enter is the one the plan uses, and the effect of a change is immediate, which makes the assumption behind a maintenance budget visible instead of buried."
    },
    {
      "q": "Is this a condition survey?",
      "a": "No. A condition survey inspects what is actually there and grades its state, which is how a real maintenance plan is built for an existing building. This planner works from specification and service life, which suits a new building or a budget. The two are complementary: the survey tells you where you are, and the planner tells you what the next twenty years look like from there."
    },
    {
      "q": "Why plan maintenance at all?",
      "a": "Because maintenance is a cost that arrives whether or not it was planned for, and an unplanned one is paid for at the worst moment. Distributing it across years, and knowing which years are heavy, allows a fund to be built rather than a crisis to be met. It also changes design decisions: a component with a short life and difficult access can be replaced before construction by one that is cheaper over twenty years."
    }
  ],
