  "/faq/carbon-footprint": [
    {
      "q": "How is embodied carbon calculated?",
      "a": "Quantity multiplied by emissions factor, per material, then summed. The quantity comes from the calculators, so a bill and a carbon figure share the same measurement. The factor comes from a published dataset and is stated per material and per unit, which means the result can be checked line by line by anyone with the same dataset."
    },
    {
      "q": "What happens to a line with no factor?",
      "a": "It is reported as uncounted and left out of the total, with the gap shown. Applying a generic factor to a material with no published value would produce a number that looks authoritative and cannot be defended, and carbon figures increasingly end up in planning documents where being wrong matters."
    },
    {
      "q": "Which stages are covered?",
      "a": "Material supply, transport to site and the construction activity itself, kept apart so each can be seen. Operational energy over the building's life is not included, and neither is end of life demolition and disposal unless they are added. That boundary is stated rather than implied, because a carbon figure without a boundary means very little."
    },
    {
      "q": "Can I compare two specifications?",
      "a": "Yes, and that is the main use. The same quantities are priced against two specifications, so the difference between a standard mix and a blended one, or between two insulation products, is a number rather than a claim. Because the quantities are identical in both runs, the difference is attributable to the substitution."
    },
    {
      "q": "Is this a certification?",
      "a": "No. It is an estimate for comparison and early design, not a certified assessment. Certification involves verification, a defined methodology, an assessor and a declared boundary, and the figures here are one input to that process rather than a substitute for it. Used that way it is genuinely useful, because most of the reduction available on a project is decided long before an assessor is appointed, at the point where the frame and the envelope are chosen."
    }
  ],
