  "/faq/circular-reuse": [
    {
      "q": "What does the recovery tool calculate?",
      "a": "The materials present, the recovery rate for each, and the resulting split between recovered and disposed tonnage. It also derives a diversion rate from those figures and shows which stream contributes most to what is lost. Re use is kept separate from recycling, since a reclaimed beam and crushed concrete are different outcomes with different claims. The tonnage split is reported alongside, because a diversion percentage on its own can look excellent while a small, awkward stream is going to landfill in its entirety."
    },
    {
      "q": "Where do recovery rates come from?",
      "a": "From published practice for each material stream, and they are rates rather than facts about a particular site. Steel is recovered at a high rate almost everywhere because its scrap value is real; concrete is recovered as aggregate where a crusher is available; gypsum recovery is limited by processing infrastructure. The rate you apply is an input, so a site with better arrangements can show it."
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does the split matter?",
      "a": "Because a single percentage hides where the loss is. A building that is mostly concrete and steel will show a high diversion rate and still lose every piece of its gypsum and most of its timber. Reporting recovered and disposed tonnage separately, per material, means the plan can target the stream that actually needs attention rather than celebrating the one that was never at risk."
    },
    {
      "q": "Can I show a client the diversion rate?",
      "a": "Yes, as a derived figure with its basis shown. That matters because diversion rates are increasingly asked for in planning and in client sustainability reporting, and a figure presented without its material split and its rates cannot be checked. Presenting the split alongside the percentage is what makes the claim defensible."
    },
    {
      "q": "Does it handle hazardous material?",
      "a": "No. Hazardous materials are flagged and excluded from the recovery calculation, because their handling is a separate regulated process. Asbestos, contaminated soil and certain finishes cannot be counted as recoverable, and including them in a diversion rate would misrepresent both the outcome and the risk."
    }
  ],
