  "/faq/contractor-credit": [
    {
      "q": "How is the credit score calculated?",
      "a": "From recorded evidence: payment history, completed work and verified references, each with a stated weight. The weights are visible, so a score can be interrogated rather than accepted, and the evidence sits beside it. Where the evidence is thin, the tool declines to score rather than producing a number that would look like knowledge it does not have."
    },
    {
      "q": "What if a contractor has no history?",
      "a": "It reports them as unscored with the reason stated. A contractor with three completed jobs and no references is an unknown, and the honest treatment of an unknown is to say so. Scoring them low would wrongly imply a negative record, and scoring them high would be inventing one."
    },
    {
      "q": "Where do the statistics come from?",
      "a": "From the records you hold and the references that have been checked. The tool does not access credit files or public registers, so the quality of a score depends on the quality of the evidence behind it. That is a limitation worth stating plainly, because a score that appears authoritative invites a decision that the evidence may not support."
    },
    {
      "q": "Who uses the score?",
      "a": "Contractors assessing a subcontractor, clients assessing a main contractor, and suppliers assessing a trade account. In each case the score is one input to a decision that also involves the size of the commitment, the payment terms and the security available. It is most useful as a structured way to look at evidence that would otherwise be assembled by impression, and as a record that the evidence was considered before the commitment was made."
    },
    {
      "q": "Does a low score mean avoid?",
      "a": "No. A low score means the evidence is thin or the record is patchy, and that calls for a different arrangement rather than an automatic refusal: staged payments, a guarantee, a smaller first package. The score is information, and treating it as a verdict would be using it for something it cannot support. A subcontractor with a patchy record on a small package may be a perfectly sound choice, and one with a clean record may be over committed elsewhere."
    }
  ],
