  "/faq/measurement-converter": [
    {
      "q": "Which units does the converter handle?",
      "a": "Length, area, volume, weight and temperature, between metric and imperial, with feet and inches accepted as a combined input. Volume conversions cover the cases that actually come up on a site, including cubic metres to cubic yards and litres to gallons. What it does not handle is a conversion from one material to another, which is a specification question rather than a unit one."
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does area conversion go wrong so often?",
      "a": "Because area and volume do not scale linearly. A metre is three point two eight feet, but a square metre is ten point seven six square feet and a cubic metre is thirty five point three one cubic feet, because each dimension carries the factor. Converting an area with the length factor gives an answer that looks plausible and is wrong by a factor of three, which is the single most common conversion error in estimating."
    },
    {
      "q": "Can I convert a material quantity?",
      "a": "Where the relationship is a physical property rather than a unit definition, yes: a bag count per cubic metre can become a bag count per cubic yard once the density is known, and a tonne can become a cubic metre of a named aggregate. Where the density is not supplied, the conversion stays unresolved rather than using a typical value that may be wrong for the material."
    },
    {
      "q": "Does it round the answer?",
      "a": "Yes, at a precision that suits the input. A dimension entered to the nearest centimetre does not become accurate to four decimal places by being converted, so the result is rounded to a sensible figure and the rounding is visible. That matters in an estimate, where a converted quantity is multiplied by a price and an over precise figure suggests a certainty the original measurement never had."
    },
    {
      "q": "Where is it most useful?",
      "a": "At the boundary between a drawing and a supplier, which is where units change hands. A plan drawn in metres quoted by a merchant in imperial sections, a specification in square feet priced in square metres, a tank specified in gallons fitted into a space measured in metres. Each of those is a place where a small error becomes a returned order or a short delivery."
    }
  ],
