  "/faq/paint-comparison": [
    {
      "q": "What does the paint comparison show?",
      "a": "Cost per square metre for each product, at its stated coverage and your coat count, alongside the coverage itself so the basis is visible. It also shows what an area you enter would cost, which turns a comparison into an order. Which paint to buy also depends on the substrate, the exposure, the colour, the sheen, the drying time and how it will look in three years, and none of those are in a coverage figure. The comparison gives you the part that can be calculated and leaves the part that cannot to you."
    },
    {
      "q": "Why compare cost per square metre?",
      "a": "Because a tin is not a unit of work. Coverage varies by a factor of two between products in the same tin size, so the shelf price tells you what a can costs and not what a wall costs. Cost per square metre combines price, coverage and coats into the figure that actually decides a painting budget, and it is the only basis on which two products can be compared at all."
    },
    {
      "q": "Where do the product figures come from?",
      "a": "From the product record, which carries the manufacturer's stated coverage per litre and the tin sizes it is sold in. Where a product has no stated coverage, the comparison reports it as incomparable rather than using a typical figure, because a comparison built on an assumed coverage is a comparison of two guesses."
    },
    {
      "q": "Does it tell me which paint to buy?",
      "a": "No. It tells you what each costs per square metre on the basis you set, which is a fact about price and coverage. Which paint to buy also depends on the substrate, the exposure, the colour, the sheen, the smell, the drying time and how it will look in three years, and none of those are in a coverage figure."
    },
    {
      "q": "Do prices include tax?",
      "a": "Prices are shown as recorded, without tax, unless a configured rule applies to your market, in which case the basis is stated on the page. That matters when comparing a trade counter price that includes tax with a supplier quote that does not, which is a comparison that produces the wrong answer if the basis is not stated."
    }
  ],
