  "/faq/increte-flooring-calculator": [
    {
      "q": "What does the decorative concrete tool measure?",
      "a": "The topping by area and thickness, the pigment or aggregate it carries, the border and pattern work by length, the sealer by area and coat, and the preparation products you specify. It does not measure the slab beneath, which has its own takeoff. Keeping them apart matters because a decorative topping is a separate trade, a separate order and a separate failure mode."
    },
    {
      "q": "Why measure topping separately from concrete?",
      "a": "Because a topping is a thin layer of a specified material over a substrate, and a slab is a structural element of a different mix with a different design. Their quantities barely relate: ten millimetres of topping over a hundred square metres is one cubic metre, while the slab under it might be fifteen. Measuring one from the other is how a decorative floor estimate ends up either wildly over or missing the finish entirely."
    },
    {
      "q": "How is a border handled?",
      "a": "By length, with its width, and as its own material line. A border is usually a contrasting mix, sometimes a different aggregate, and it needs its own quantity rather than being absorbed into the main area. Saw cut patterns are lengths as well, and each metre of cut is a metre of joint that has to be filled and finished."
    },
    {
      "q": "Is the substrate included?",
      "a": "The substrate is not included, and the preparation of it is only included where you enter it. A topping needs a sound, clean, correctly prepared substrate, and the preparation is a real cost that varies enormously with what is there. The tool asks for the preparation you specify rather than assuming a clean new slab, because the assumption is what turns a quote into a loss. A substrate that has been down for years will need grinding, filling or a primer, and each of those has a coverage and a cost that belongs in the order."
    },
    {
      "q": "Does it allow for waste?",
      "a": "Yes, as a percentage you set. Decorative work produces waste for a reason ordinary flooring does not: colour consistency comes from the same batch, and a border that does not meet cleanly is a return visit. The percentage is a line rather than a hidden loading, so its size can be seen and agreed before the job starts."
    }
  ],
