  "/faq/wall-finish-estimator": [
    {
      "q": "What does the wall finish estimator cover?",
      "a": "Plaster and render by area and thickness, ceilings on their own line, beads and reveals by length, and the preparation products you specify. Screed and the bagged alternatives are handled at their own stated yields. What it does not do is decide which finish a wall needs, because that follows from the substrate, the room and the standard required."
    },
    {
      "q": "Why is thickness an input?",
      "a": "Because material follows volume, and volume is area multiplied by thickness. Doubling the thickness doubles the sand and cement on the same wall, and the difference between a ten and an eighteen millimetre render is a lorry load rather than a rounding. Thickness also decides whether a single coat will do, which changes the labour as well as the material, and it is the first thing a specification changes when a standard is raised."
    },
    {
      "q": "Are ceilings included?",
      "a": "Yes, as their own surface with their own finish. A ceiling is usually a different mix from a wall, applied by a different technique, and it may be a different trade. Measuring it separately means its quantity can be ordered and priced on its own, and it also means a change to the ceiling specification does not disturb the walls."
    },
    {
      "q": "Does it account for openings?",
      "a": "Yes, by dimension, before the volume is calculated. A door or a window is an area removed from the wall, and the deduction happens at the area stage so the thickness is applied to what is actually covered. What is not deducted is the bead or reveal around the opening, which is counted as a length instead, since that is the detail the opening creates."
    },
    {
      "q": "What about corner beads and reveals?",
      "a": "Corner beads and reveals are counted by length from the details you list, and priced as their own line. They are the part of a plastering estimate most often left out, because they are small items that follow from the geometry rather than from the area. A room with eight external corners needs eight times the bead of a room with one, and the area does not show it."
    }
  ],
