  "/faq/blueprint-takeoff": [
    {
      "q": "How does the plan takeoff work?",
      "a": "You read the dimensions from the drawing and enter them by element; the tool organises them, calculates the quantities they imply and keeps the source dimension beside each result. That is a takeoff in the traditional sense, with the arithmetic done by the same engine as the standalone calculators. The judgement about what the drawing shows stays with the person reading it."
    },
    {
      "q": "Can it read a PDF for me?",
      "a": "It can read a drawing as a document, and it will not measure it. Automatic measurement requires interpreting line weights, scales and layers, and the failures are silent: a wall taken from the wrong layer, a door counted twice at a revision cloud, a dimension string that was superseded. A figure entered by someone who looked at the drawing can be argued with, which is worth more than speed on the numbers that decide a price."
    },
    {
      "q": "Why not measure the drawing automatically?",
      "a": "Because a wrong quantity that looks precise is worse than a right one entered slowly. A takeoff is a set of judgements about what a drawing shows, and those judgements are exactly what a person is qualified to make and a model is not. The tool takes the result of that judgement and removes the arithmetic errors, which is where most takeoff mistakes actually come from."
    },
    {
      "q": "Which quantities does it produce?",
      "a": "Quantities by element: wall areas and the units they require, concrete volumes, roof areas, openings as a schedule, and the lengths that trim and services are measured in. Each is calculated from the dimensions entered, and each carries that dimension, so an element can be recomputed by hand if a figure is disputed."
    },
    {
      "q": "Is the drawing stored?",
      "a": "No. The drawing stays where it is. What the tool stores is the dimensions you read and the quantities they produced, which is the takeoff. That matters for a client's confidentiality and for a practice's own record: a takeoff that can be shown without the drawing beside it is often what a subcontractor is given, and a takeoff that carries the drawing is a document that cannot be sent anywhere."
    }
  ],
