  "/faq/global-site-safety-estimator": [
    {
      "q": "Does this estimator certify that my site complies with OSHA, HSE or ISO requirements?",
      "a": "No. The page multiplies your inputs by ratios you can see and prints the framework references that describe the relevant duty. A risk assessment, a method statement, a training record and an emergency plan are separate documents produced by people who know the site, and none of them can be generated from a perimeter length. Treat the export as a procurement schedule that starts a conversation with your safety adviser."
    },
    {
      "q": "Why doesn't the tool automatically use a 6 ft or 2 m fall threshold?",
      "a": "Because the trigger height depends on jurisdiction, task and exposure, and treating one figure as universal produces confident nonsense. The estimator asks you to mark where work at height happens and how many workers need protection, then counts equipment for that number. Where local rules set a threshold, your safety professional applies it when deciding how many workers to enter. The tool stays out of that judgement on purpose."
    },
    {
      "q": "How are perimeter signs calculated?",
      "a": "Boundary length divided by your spacing assumption, rounded up. A one hundred and twenty metre boundary at ten metre spacing gives twelve signs, and at fifteen metres it gives eight. The count is a purchasing figure. Sight lines, gates, corners, pedestrian routes and local sign rules decide where those signs actually stand, and a competent person should walk the boundary before any are fixed."
    },
    {
      "q": "Are first-aid kit ratios regulatory requirements?",
      "a": "No universal ratio is claimed, and none is invented. You enter the planning allowance you work to and the quantity follows. First-aid cover depends on workforce size, shift patterns, distance from medical care, the hazards present and the training of nominated responders, all of which sit outside anything a calculator can see. The line exists so the item is not forgotten when the order is placed."
    },
    {
      "q": "What does ISO 7010 contribute?",
      "a": "It defines the registered symbols used on safety signs, which helps you specify the right family of sign for each message. Adoption and enforcement come from local law and client requirements, not from the standard by itself. The estimator names the framework beside the sign line so the specification can be checked, and it does not reproduce or approve artwork, since sign graphics have to match the edition of the standard in force where the site sits."
    }
  ],
