  "/faq/material-advisor": [
    {
      "q": "What does the material advisor do?",
      "a": "It takes the conditions you describe and returns materials that suit them, with the reasoning stated for each and the unsuitable ones flagged. It is a narrowing tool: it reduces a specification choice to a shortlist with reasons, which is where a specifier's time is best spent. A shortlist of three with reasons can be argued with, priced and specified; a catalogue of forty cannot. It does not produce a design, a fixing detail or a price."
    },
    {
      "q": "What does it base the advice on?",
      "a": "On the conditions you enter: climate, exposure, position in the building, use of the space and the maintenance the owner is prepared to accept. Each of those changes the answer, and changing one on the page changes the shortlist, which is the quickest way to see whether a recommendation actually rests on the condition you think it does."
    },
    {
      "q": "Does it price anything?",
      "a": "No. It names materials and their suitability. Pricing comes from the market price book or from figures you enter, and a material that suits a condition may be unavailable or uneconomic locally. Keeping suitability and cost apart is deliberate, because a shortlist that has already been filtered by price hides the options that would have been right. Price it afterwards, when the list is short enough to price properly."
    },
    {
      "q": "Is this engineering advice?",
      "a": "No. It is guidance on selection. Engineering advice involves loads, movement, fixings, substrate and the specific detail, and it carries responsibility that a selection tool cannot. Use the advisor to narrow a list and an engineer or a specifier to design with it. The two are complements, and the handover between them is much cheaper than a redesign after a failure."
    },
    {
      "q": "What about availability locally?",
      "a": "It flags availability as a separate consideration rather than modelling it, because local stock, lead times and merchant coverage change weekly and by location. Where availability decides a specification, the answer comes from a merchant rather than from a page, and the advisor says so instead of presenting a material that cannot be bought."
    }
  ],
