  "/faq/regional-cost-index": [
    {
      "q": "What does the regional index do?",
      "a": "A relative factor, by region and by trade, applied to a measured estimate. Labour and material factors are separate because they move for different reasons, so a labour heavy job adjusts differently from a material heavy one. The base and the adjusted figures stay side by side, which means the adjustment can be seen rather than absorbed."
    },
    {
      "q": "Where do the indexes come from?",
      "a": "From recorded regional differentials, which are relative rather than absolute prices. That matters because a factor survives a change in the base estimate, while a set of local prices would have to be replaced every time a quantity changed. The date of the factors is recorded, since a differential narrows or widens over time."
    },
    {
      "q": "Does it convert currency too?",
      "a": "No, and deliberately not. A currency conversion and a regional cost difference are different adjustments, and applying both through one factor produces a number that mixes two movements. The regional index adjusts for where the work is, and a currency conversion is applied afterwards at a stated rate, so each change can be seen and questioned on its own."
    },
    {
      "q": "Why not just use one national price?",
      "a": "Because a national figure is a fiction that is convenient for a spreadsheet. Labour in one region can differ from another by a third, and haulage and local levies add further variation. A contractor pricing a job a long way from home needs to know what the local cost base is, and a client comparing two locations needs the same. The regional index gives both a way to have that conversation with a number in front of them rather than a hunch about the other side of the country."
    },
    {
      "q": "Can I apply it to a saved estimate?",
      "a": "Yes, where the estimate is held and the region is known. The factor is applied to the measured quantities rather than to the total, so a change of region produces a re priced estimate rather than a scaled number. That keeps the traceability of the original lines and makes the regional adjustment one more visible step rather than a mystery. Where a client queries the difference between two locations, the answer is a factor and a date rather than an apology."
    }
  ],
