  "/faq/bim-ifc-import": [
    {
      "q": "What does the IFC import do?",
      "a": "It reads the file, recognises the element types it understands, maps them to the categories the calculators use, and extracts the quantities the geometry supports. Elements it cannot map are listed with their type rather than discarded. The output is a takeoff by element that can be traced back to the model, which is the point of importing rather than re measuring. On a coordinated model that saves a week; on an uncoordinated one it tells you what has to be fixed before it can be used at all."
    },
    {
      "q": "What happens to elements it cannot map?",
      "a": "They are listed as unmapped with their type, so the reason is visible. Usually it is a modelling decision: an element extruded as a generic form, a wall without a classification, a family that has no equivalent. Those are fixable in the authoring tool, and reporting them converts a silent gap in a takeoff into a task for the modeller."
    },
    {
      "q": "Are the model quantities trusted directly?",
      "a": "No. The model's quantities are shown beside the extracted ones so a difference can be seen, but the takeoff is calculated from the geometry by the same engine as the standalone calculators. A model can carry a quantity that was typed rather than derived, and trusting it would import someone else's arithmetic error into an estimate that otherwise has none."
    },
    {
      "q": "Which quantities come through?",
      "a": "Wall areas and the thicknesses behind them, slab and footing volumes, roof areas at the modelled pitch, run lengths for pipe and conduit, and openings as a schedule with sizes. Those are the quantities the geometry supports reliably. Anything that depends on a specification, such as a block size or a mix, comes from the properties where they exist and is reported as missing where they do not."
    },
    {
      "q": "Does it validate the model?",
      "a": "No. Validation is a separate discipline: coordination, clashes, model completeness and standards compliance are checks against a brief, and a quantity import does none of them. What the import does is tell you which elements it could not use, which is a partial and useful signal about model quality rather than an assessment of it."
    }
  ],
