  "/faq/field-sync": [
    {
      "q": "What does field sync do?",
      "a": "It brings a measurement taken on site into the estimate or takeoff it belongs to, recording where it came from and when. Where the figure differs from what is stored, it reports the conflict rather than choosing. The result is an estimate that reflects site reality and a history that shows how it got there."
    },
    {
      "q": "What happens if a sync clashes with a stored figure?",
      "a": "It reports both and asks. The stored figure has a source, usually a drawing or an earlier measurement, and the synced one has a source too. Choosing automatically would discard one of them without a record, and the discarded one is often the evidence someone later needs. The resolution is a person's decision, recorded with its reason."
    },
    {
      "q": "Does it work offline?",
      "a": "It queues the change and sends it when a connection returns, which is how a site actually works: basements and new builds have no signal. A queued record shows as pending rather than as sent, so nobody assumes a figure is in the office when it is still on a phone. That distinction is small and it is the difference between an estimate that is current and one that is quietly stale. A pending record is visible, so somebody can chase it; a silently lost one becomes a phone call from a site that has already ordered the wrong quantity."
    },
    {
      "q": "Is it a project management tool?",
      "a": "No. It moves measurements between a site and an estimate, which is one specific flow of data. Scheduling, task assignment, progress tracking and document control are a project management system, and this is not one. Use it for the dimensions that decide quantities, and use the right tool for the rest."
    },
    {
      "q": "Who can see a synced record?",
      "a": "The people on the job it belongs to, and the record keeps its source. A synced measurement is part of an estimate, so it is visible to whoever can see that estimate, which is the scope that makes sense: a site measurement is not public data, and it is not private to a phone either."
    }
  ],
