  "/faq/material-prices": [
    {
      "q": "What does the material price page show?",
      "a": "Materials with their price, the unit it is quoted in, the market it applies to and the date it was recorded. That is a record rather than a quote, and the difference is the date: a price with a date can be judged for age, and a price without one cannot be judged at all. The page is deliberately boring about this, because an estimate depends on it."
    },
    {
      "q": "Are these prices guaranteed?",
      "a": "No. They are recorded prices, which means they are what was known when they were entered, in the market they belong to. A price can change the day after it is recorded, and a supplier can quote something different for a particular order. The page gives a starting figure with a date so you can decide whether to use it or to get a quote for the job."
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does the date matter?",
      "a": "Because material prices move, sometimes sharply, and a figure with no date cannot be assessed. A bag of cement recorded last month may still be right and may be ten percent out, and the only way to know is to see when it was recorded. The age of a price is a better guide to its reliability than the price itself."
    },
    {
      "q": "Can I see where a price came from?",
      "a": "Every entry keeps its source and its date, and an estimate that used it can be traced back to it. That matters when a client asks why a reissued quote differs, or when a job is compared against a similar one from six months earlier. The answer is a figure, a market and a date, rather than a recollection."
    },
    {
      "q": "How does this relate to the calculators?",
      "a": "The calculators read from the book for the market selected, and where no price exists for a material in that market, the calculator asks for a figure instead of substituting one. That keeps a price in a quote traceable to either the book or to you, which is the only basis on which a total can be defended line by line."
    }
  ],
