  "/faq/borehole-water-system": [
    {
      "q": "How is the overhead tank and its stand sized?",
      "a": "The stand is sized from the load, not from a picture of one. A litre of water weighs a kilogram, so a 5,000 litre tank carries five tonnes before the tank shell, the ring beam, the deck and the piers are counted. That load is divided between the piers, and each pier gets a footing pad sized so the pressure on the ground stays inside the allowable bearing value. The tank sits on a reinforced concrete ring beam the full width of its base, because point loading a plastic tank on four block corners is how bases crack. Above 12 times the pier thickness the tool warns that a freestanding pier is getting slender, and past the BS 5628 limit of 27 it tells you to pour columns instead."
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does the stand need wind and footing checks?",
      "a": "Because the tower is the part that fails. A tank full of water six metres up behaves like a sail in wind and like a weight on whatever ground is under it, and those are two different checks. The wind check uses a basic wind speed for the market or your own figure, turns it into pressure, applies it to the tank and the stand, and compares the overturning moment with the resistance of the stand with the tank empty, at a required factor of safety of 1.5. The ground check compares the loaded pad pressure with the allowable bearing figure, which is a presumption unless you enter a test result."
    },
    {
      "q": "How deep should the submersible pump sit?",
      "a": "Set it below the pumping water level, not the static level, because the level falls while the pump runs. The tool estimates drawdown from the safe yield and the aquifer, adds it to the static level, and sets the pump about five metres below the result. It also checks the cased depth, because a pump cannot sit in an uncased hole, and it warns if the setting leaves less than two metres of submergence, if the pump lands at or below the top of the screen, or if the cased section is too shallow to place it where it should go. Those warnings change the drilling instruction, which is why they are printed instead of being silently corrected."
    },
    {
      "q": "How much tank storage does a house need?",
      "a": "Enough to cover the peak and survive an outage. The estimator takes your occupants and consumption tier, works out the daily demand, multiplies the average hourly rate by a peak factor for the morning rush, and measures that against what the pump delivers in the same hour. The shortfall over the rush window is the buffer. On top of it sits an emergency reserve of a day or more, because pumps fail, power goes and tanks run dry at the worst time. The total is then rounded to a tank that is actually sold not a theoretical volume nobody can order."
    },
    {
      "q": "What is left for a person to decide?",
      "a": "It leaves out the things that need a person on site. The strata, the specific capacity and the water quality are modelled from what you enter, so a driller's log and a laboratory assay replace them. The stand is priced by quantity and checked against simple limits, not designed and signed: the ring beam reinforcement, the pad mesh and the column cage still need an engineer. Prices, tax and labour come from your price book, and where a market has no verified figure the tool does not invent one."
    }
  ],
