  "/faq/window-blinds": [
    {
      "q": "How is a window blind priced per window and per square metre?",
      "a": "Because shops quote both, and the calculator applies both. The rate per square metre is the headline figure on the wall. The minimum charge per window is what stops a small opening being sold below cost, so it overrides the rate whenever the measured area prices lower. Fabric is then cut with an allowance, because the drop has to clear the hardware and a pattern has to line up. A four by four opening therefore rarely costs what four by four multiplied by the metre rate suggests. The page shows the rate, the charged area, the minimum and whether it applied, so the difference between the two numbers is visible, not mysterious."
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does a small window cost almost as much as a large one?",
      "a": "Only the minimum charge explains most of it. A quarter of the fabric still needs the same headrail, the same brackets, the same cutting, the same van, the same fitter and the same return visit, and those costs do not shrink with the window. Once the minimum takes over, two windows of different sizes can cost the same. That is also why a whole house priced per window looks cheaper than the same house priced per square metre: the minimum is doing the work on every small opening. The calculator keeps the minimum separate from the rate for exactly that reason, so you can see where a supplier is charging for material and where they are charging for the visit."
    },
    {
      "q": "What measurements do I need before ordering blinds?",
      "a": "The finished opening, not the glass. Measure the width across the reveal and the drop from the head to the sill or the floor, in the same unit for both, and measure more than one window if they look alike, because they rarely are. Blinds need a small clearance inside a reveal, and an outside fit adds to both dimensions, so decide which you are doing before you measure. Then decide the control side, because a chain on the wrong side is a return visit. The calculator takes width, drop, count and type per opening and does the rest, and it converts feet to metres before pricing, so a four by four is 1.49 square metres whichever unit you typed."
    },
    {
      "q": "What changes the price between blind types?",
      "a": "The type first, then the grade. A rigid slat, a folded fabric and a woven natural blind are different products with different waste allowances and different minimums, so the type moves the price further than a few centimetres of window. Grade is the second lever: premium prints and heavier fabric sell at a large multiple of standard stock, and the calculator applies that as a multiplier on the rate. Motorisation is the third, and it is not a percentage, which is why the calculator asks for the motor figure instead of inventing one. Fitting is the fourth and the most predictable, because it is charged per window and does not care what the fabric cost."
    },
    {
      "q": "Can I price blinds outside Nigeria?",
      "a": "Yes, and the rules do not change. The measurement, the waste allowance, the minimum charge and the fitting are universal; what changes is the rate, so the calculator opens in any market and asks for the local rate where it has no verified band, then prices the schedule with the same arithmetic. What it leaves out is a site survey, a colour and fabric decision, a motor figure, and tax. It also leaves out the awkward openings: a bay, a sloping head, a door with a handle to clear. Those need a person with a tape and a sample book, and the calculator is honest about stopping where measurement stops."
    }
  ],
